Wait — parseISO8601ToLocalTimeInMillis with LocalDateTime.parse: the formatISO8601 output includes an offset (e.g. "+0000" or "-0800"). LocalDateTime.parse with a formatter that contains an offset pattern Z — can LocalDateTime parse a string with an offset? The pattern Z parses an offset field, but LocalDateTime.parse requires only local date-time fields... When parsing, the formatter extracts OffsetSeconds too; converting to LocalDateTime — `LocalDateTime.parse(text, formatter)` calls `formatter.parse(text, LocalDateTime::from)`. LocalDateTime.from(TemporalAccessor) requires NANO_OF_DAY and EPOCH_DAY... Actually LocalDateTime.from requires ChronoField fields; the parsed result would contain offset but LocalDateTime.from might work since date and time fields are present. Yes, LocalDateTime.from(TemporalAccessor) only needs EPOCH_DAY and NANO_OF_DAY, extra offset field is ignored. So parsing works, but then `.atZone(ZoneId.systemDefault())` ignores the parsed offset! That breaks round-trip correctness for the offset: formatISO8601 outputs with system-default zone offset, so round-trip in the same zone works. But semantically, if input has a different offset, we'd be wrong vs SimpleDateFormat which respects the offset.

Better: parse respecting the offset. Use ZonedDateTime.parse — but if input lacks offset, that fails. SimpleDateFormat with Z pattern is lenient about missing fields too (defaults epoch 1970 etc.). Hmm.

